<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Man Singh II of Jaipur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sawai Man Singh II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Man Singh II was the last ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, from 1922 to 1949. He merged his state into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-state-of-greater-rajasthan">Greater Rajasthan</a> in 1949, with Jaipur as capital, and served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> until 1956. A celebrated polo player, he later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. His third wife was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gayatri-devi">Gayatri Devi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/man-singh-ii-of-jaipur.jpg" alt="Man Singh II of Jaipur" width="315" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bassano Ltd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Man_Singh_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
